  {
    "pkattachmentId": 49247,
    "bookid": 44,
    "chapterId": 122,
    "fileId": 17520,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "This adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma in an adult is composed of cords and trabeculae of cells punctuated by irregularly distributed clumps of wet keratin with superimposed calcification.",
    "source": "Bette K. Kleinschmidt-DeMasters",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/44/122/17520.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/44/122/thumb_17520.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 49248,
    "bookid": 44,
    "chapterId": 122,
    "fileId": 17521,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "A characteristic feature of adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma but not papillary craniopharyngioma is the presence of wet keratin, which is composed of anucleate ghost cells. A second characteristic feature limited to this type is a concentration of cells at the perimeter of the epithelial trabeculae, i.e. tumour palisading.",
    "source": "Bette K. Kleinschmidt-DeMasters",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/44/122/17521.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/44/122/thumb_17521.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 49249,
    "bookid": 44,
    "chapterId": 122,
    "fileId": 17522,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Although cytologically bland, adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma can be clinically aggressive because of its tendency to invade into surrounding eloquent neural tissue, eliciting intense reactive gliosis and Rosenthal fibres. Tumours show oval nuclei with an absence of nuclear pleomorphism and modest amounts of cytoplasm. Note that the calcification occurs within the wet keratin not the epithelium. Note also the mucinous cysts throughout the epithelium; these can coalesce to form larger cysts.",
    "source": "Bette K. Kleinschmidt-DeMasters",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/44/122/17522.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/44/122/thumb_17522.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 49250,
    "bookid": 44,
    "chapterId": 122,
    "fileId": 17523,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "In some examples, the tumour architecture forms ribbon-like strands of cells with few (if any) areas of wet keratin.",
    "source": "Bette K. Kleinschmidt-DeMasters",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/44/122/17523.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/44/122/thumb_17523.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 49251,
    "bookid": 44,
    "chapterId": 122,
    "fileId": 17524,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Cytologically, the tumour shows relatively monotonous features, with cells showing bland oval nuclei devoid of conspicuous nucleoli or pleomorphism, modest amounts of cytoplasm, and only infrequent mitotic figures. Ki-67 immunolabelling is preferentially concentrated in the basal peripheral palisading layer.",
    "source": "Bette K. Kleinschmidt-DeMasters",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/44/122/17524.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/44/122/thumb_17524.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 49252,
    "bookid": 44,
    "chapterId": 122,
    "fileId": 17526,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Rarely, a craniopharyngioma will show focal areas with ciliation; usually the remainder of the tumour is a papillary craniopharyngioma, but this is an exceptional case where adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma coexisted with the ciliated area. It is debated whether this a transitional lesion (the so-called ciliated craniopharyngioma) or simply represents a separate, coexistent Rathke cleft cyst.",
    "source": "Bette K. Kleinschmidt-DeMasters",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/44/122/17526.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/44/122/thumb_17526.jpg"
  },
